### Time Value of Money
    TVM:
        related to consumption timing: investing financial assets allows storing wealth and postponing consumption as well as borrowing and bringing consumption forward
        Difference in value of money today and in the future is called TVM
            by investing our savings in a risk free asset today, we can convert our present money to future money (interest rates allows us ot convert money from one point to another)
    The Rate
    effective appreciation of money over time, assuming that the investor does not withdraw any money
    Reward for delaying consumption
        the further in the future, the higher the FV of the money
    Effective Annual Rate (EAR) vs Annual Percentage rates
        APRs are rates quoted by banks along with frequency of compounding
        APR can be converted to any periodic rate by dividing by the frequency of compounding
            Quart: APR/4, Monthly APR/12, Semi-annual APR/2
            the power of the rate is how many times it is compounded so multiply by 12 for months and others accordingly
        EAR is the interest rate that would come out if it was compounded once a year
            EX: .5% monthly interest --> EAR = (1+.005)^12 - 1 = 6.17%
            EX2: 20% interest over 3 years --> EAR = (1+.2)^(1/3) - 1 = 6.265%
    Nominal vs Real Rate
        Difference is the rate of inflation --> general increase in prices
        Real rate of return = nominal rate of return (EAR) - inflation
        capture the appreciation in the value of goods and services that can be consumed
    Risk
        Risk and return
            risk refers to possibility that realized cash flows will be different from cash flows expected at the same time of the decision making
            main risk is default (non-payment)
        Investor Rationality
            investor is risk averse and expects compensation for facing risk
            Expected return on a risky asset > return on the risk-free asset
            compensation is the risk premium
            Risk premium = [expected return on an asset bearing a particular set of risks] – [risk-free return]
            The higher the risk, the greater the risk premium demanded by rational investors.
            In Finance we assume that financial markets are populated by rational investors.
        Return on risk-free asset
            A risk-free asset carries no uncertainty about future payments and the expected return will be realized under all conditions (E(R) = r risk free)
## Lecture 2
### Risk and Return
    a rational investor ranks investments by risk: demand greater returns for investing in riskier assets
    If financial markets consist of rational investors, then asset price reflect the expected risk
    Expected return on an investment depends on risk
        risk is the possibility that the expected return != realized return
        a distribution with a higher variance will be riskier when the expected value is about the same
    Risky assets have uncertain returns
        when comparing assets, eliminate anything that has a lower expected value and a higher SD than than another option
        Can rank options by dividing expected return by SD, this would give expected return per unit of risk
        Expected return = probability weighted average return
### Arbitrage and the Law of One Price
    Law of One Price
        Financial markets have many traders who engage in trading for profit
        Arbitrage: opportunity to make a profit without assuming risk or committing your own capital by taking advantage of price differentials across markets
            EX: suppose gold price in London is $135 per gram and $145 per gram in Chicago, would buy in London and then sell it in Chicago
                in reality it is not riskless because prices could change instantly
        Why do arbitrageurs borrow money?
            they exploit tiny differences in markets populated by high numbers of investors
            markets can move against them quickly
                they have to discover the inefficiency fast and act on it quick before it closes
        Competitive markets
            markets that are characterized by large number of buyers and sellers who can't influence the market price
        Arbitrage and LOOP
            Due to arbitrageurs trading in high volumes, in competitive markets asset prices converge very quickly across different trading venues
            the more the activity the quicker things will converge
            No-arbitrage price: price at which arbitrageurs do not make a profit on arbitrage trading
            Law of One price: If equivalent investment opportunities trade simultaneously in different competitive markets, then they must trade for the same price in all market
### Diversification
    diversification in stock portfolios
        common vs independent risks and diversification
            in large portfolio the number of independent events is relatively predictable
                hence, holding a large portfolio of theft insurance allows the insurance company to price insurance policies with high precision
                common risks can't be reduced by diversification
        firm-specific vs market risk
            when many stock are combined in large portfolio, firm-specific risks for each stock will average out and be diversified
            the systematic (market) risk affects all firms and will not be diversified (a specific industry)
            firm specific risk: affect single firm or set of firms -- stock return reaction to firm-specific news
            market risk: affects the entire stock market -- stock return to market wide news
    Portfolio volatility of type S and I stocks
        Type S stocks only carry systematic (market) risk, it can't be eliminated therefore portfolio volatility remains the same regardless of the number of stocks
        Type I firms stocks only idiosyncratic (firm-specific) risk, therefore portfolio volatility decrease with the number of stocks in the portfolio
    Smart investors diversify
        In consequence:
            risk premium for firm-specific diversifiable risk is zero, so investors are not compensated for holding firm-specific risk
            it follows that the risk premium of a security is determined by its systematic risk and doesn't depend on its diversifiable risk
### Asset Value Principles
    Cost of capital
        required rate of return by investors in an asset is the cost of capital
        investors who invest in that asset want to make at least as much money on that investment as they could on an alternative investment with similar risk
        cost of equity: rate of return required by shareholders
        cost of debt: rat eof return required by creditors
    Two main approaches
        Discounted cash flow and relative valuation models
        DCF:
            quantify expected future cash flows (future values)
            discount them by the cost of equity (estimated present value)
            find the intrinsic or fair value of an asset (sum of estimated present values)
            Principle: in long term temporary price fluctuations offset, the fundamental share price acts as a magnet or core, around which the actual share price will fluctuate
        Relative Valuation:
            multiples-based or comparable based models
            according to LOOP: identical goods must sell for the same price
            Logic: can determine whether an asset is over or undervalued based on comparison of key value metrics to those of similar assets
            Principle: the market mistakes in overvaluing or undervaluing assets relative to similar peers => these mistakes will be corrected in a short time.
    Dividend discount model as a DCF model
        shareholders as a group will receive dividends to infinity
        shareholders' expected return is the cost of equity (discount rate commensurate with the risk they assume)
        price today is the discounted stream of infinite dividends with the cost of equity as the discount rate
            dividends can be constant forever
            dividends can grow at predetermined rate
            Price(today) = P0 = Div1/(cost of equity - growth rate) =  Div0(1+growth rate)/ (cost of equity - growth rate)
                Div 1 = Div 0 (1+g) or div1 will be called the next year dividend
    Selection of comparable firms
        tradeoffs between sample size and precision of the match
        top-down approach can avoid selection bias
            1. largest sample size possible
                all relevant-- size, book to market
            2. smaller group -- exclude companies based on objective criteria
                main geographic or product markets
            3. closest peers -- capital structure, product markets, life-cycles
                same industry, same markets, detailed analysis
    P/E model
        EX: suppose a firm has a forward looking EPS of $2, what is the share price if comparable firms trade at forward looking avg p/e ratio of 25. Estimated price is $50
        lots of assumptions made with this: both have similar: mix of products and markets, market shares, growth rates, capital structure, dividend payout ratios, financial and accounting policies
    Inherent flaws in RV models
        In RV models there is no clear guidance about how to adjust for differences in business models, expected future growth rates, risk, or financial and accounting policies
        Further, comparables only provide information regarding the value of a firm relative to other firms in the comparison set using multiples will not help us determine if an entire industry is over- or
        undervalued
        P/E are also changing constantly so it is hard to pin down a single one
        Theses models have to be short term because of the fluctuations

### Concept of Net Present Value
    Future profits and cash flows
        capital budgeting involves the set of decisions that determine corporate investments in real assets
    Capital budgeting steps:
        1. estimate the investment needs of the project at present
        2. estimate future free cash flows generated by the project
        3. discount these future free cash flows to present by the opportunity cost capital
        4. sum up the pv of all outgoing and incoming FCF to arrive at NPV of the project
            NPV = contribution of this activity to firm value
    NPV
        Compares PV of FCF from the investments (benefits) to PV of costs of the project
    NPV decision rule
        reject project with negative NPV and when making an investment decision choose the project with higher NPV
        0 NPV is a project where the firm does not benefit or lose money from the project
### Financial Market Efficiency
    core importance for investors, corporations, and financial decision makers
    If financial markets are efficient:
        1. aggregate buy and sell decisions by investors result in asset prices that correctly reflect the risk and return characteristics of investing in these assets
            in other words: asset prices become informative about the underlying risk and return expectations hence the true value of assets
        2. in long term no investor can beat the market by identifying under and overvalued asserts and implementing profitable trades
        3. hence, the best investment strategy is to buy and hold the market
    Logic of Efficient market hypothesis
        1. Financial markets are competitive
        2. information is cheap and widely available
        3. Due to free or cheap trading, asset prices adjust fully and quickly to new information
        Outcome: prices fully reflect available information; therefore, financial markets are efficient
